``` BYOD Ransomware and Extortion: Vanderbilt University Attack
    Field names follow CIM/UDM conventions and have not been tested against live data; adjust them to your environment.
    Splunk hunt, CIM data models (tstats). Author: daniyyell-dev. https://github.com/daniyyell-dev/rapid-threat-intel/tree/main/threats/2026-11_November-2026/2026-11-10/BYOD-Ransomware-Vanderbilt-University-Attack
    Sources: https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/vanderbilt-university-hit-in-byod-ransomware-attack-data-theft-claimed/ ; https://www.ransomware.live/group/BYOD ; https://nashvillebanner.com/2026/11/09/vanderbilt-university-cyberattack-byod/
    Published 2026-11-09. Set the time picker to at least the last 30 days, or back to the earliest activity in the sources.
    The indicators below are live and malicious: do not browse to them.
    Needs these CIM data models populated: Network_Resolution, Web, Email, Endpoint. A model with no data simply returns nothing.
    summariesonly=false also reads events the acceleration has not summarised yet. If these data models are
    accelerated, summariesonly=true is much faster but only sees the summary range.
    No CIM? Use hunt_raw.spl with the rti_* macros from all/splunk_macros.conf.
    Output: first_seen, last_seen, ioc, ioc_type, data_model, host, user, src, dest, detail, count

    Behaviours (indicators of attack) are extra sections below, with ioc_type "behaviour (alert)" or "behaviour (hunt)".
    Behaviours are leads, not confirmed indicators, and can match legitimate activity. Compare any hit against your own baseline and environment before acting.
    Behaviours are for hunting and alerting only; never block on them. ```
| tstats summariesonly=false fillnull_value="-" count min(_time) AS first_seen max(_time) AS last_seen
    FROM datamodel=Network_Resolution.DNS
    WHERE DNS.query IN ("byodxn5s7sua6oyyjj74rycgn6afncfckprt5ezskt3cni6lg3wdrgad.onion", "*.byodxn5s7sua6oyyjj74rycgn6afncfckprt5ezskt3cni6lg3wdrgad.onion")
    BY DNS.src DNS.dest DNS.query DNS.reply_code
| rename "DNS.*" AS *
| rex field=query "(?i)(?<ioc>(?:byodxn5s7sua6oyyjj74rycgn6afncfckprt5ezskt3cni6lg3wdrgad\.onion))\.?$"
| eval data_model="Network_Resolution", ioc_type="domain", host=src, user="-", detail="DNS query ".query." (".reply_code.")"
| append [
    | tstats summariesonly=false fillnull_value="-" count min(_time) AS first_seen max(_time) AS last_seen
        FROM datamodel=Web.Web
        WHERE (Web.url IN ("*://byodxn5s7sua6oyyjj74rycgn6afncfckprt5ezskt3cni6lg3wdrgad.onion*", "*.byodxn5s7sua6oyyjj74rycgn6afncfckprt5ezskt3cni6lg3wdrgad.onion*", "byodxn5s7sua6oyyjj74rycgn6afncfckprt5ezskt3cni6lg3wdrgad.onion*") OR Web.dest IN ("byodxn5s7sua6oyyjj74rycgn6afncfckprt5ezskt3cni6lg3wdrgad.onion", "*.byodxn5s7sua6oyyjj74rycgn6afncfckprt5ezskt3cni6lg3wdrgad.onion"))
        BY Web.src Web.user Web.dest Web.url Web.http_user_agent Web.status
    | rename "Web.*" AS *
    | rex field=url "(?i)(?<ioc>byodxn5s7sua6oyyjj74rycgn6afncfckprt5ezskt3cni6lg3wdrgad\.onion)"
    | eval ioc=coalesce(ioc, dest), data_model="Web", ioc_type=if(match(ioc, "/"), "url", "domain"), host=src, detail="HTTP ".status." ".url." UA: ".http_user_agent
]
| append [
    | tstats summariesonly=false fillnull_value="-" count min(_time) AS first_seen max(_time) AS last_seen
        FROM datamodel=Email.All_Email
        WHERE (All_Email.url IN ("*://byodxn5s7sua6oyyjj74rycgn6afncfckprt5ezskt3cni6lg3wdrgad.onion*", "*.byodxn5s7sua6oyyjj74rycgn6afncfckprt5ezskt3cni6lg3wdrgad.onion*", "byodxn5s7sua6oyyjj74rycgn6afncfckprt5ezskt3cni6lg3wdrgad.onion*"))
        BY All_Email.src_user All_Email.recipient All_Email.subject All_Email.file_name All_Email.file_hash All_Email.url
    | rename "All_Email.*" AS *
    | eval matched=file_hash." ".url
    | rex field=matched "(?i)(?<ioc>byodxn5s7sua6oyyjj74rycgn6afncfckprt5ezskt3cni6lg3wdrgad\.onion)"
    | eval data_model="Email", ioc_type=case(match(ioc, "^[0-9a-fA-F]{64}$"), "sha256", match(ioc, "^[0-9a-fA-F]{40}$"), "sha1", match(ioc, "^[0-9a-fA-F]{32}$"), "md5", match(ioc, "/"), "url", 1==1, "domain"), host="-", user=recipient, src=src_user, dest=recipient, detail="email '".subject."' ".file_name
]

``` Behaviour 1 (Alert): README_BYOD ransom notes
    Look for: Ransom notes named README_BYOD_<number>.txt created on many hosts or shares in a short time
    Log source: EDR file events, file server auditing
    Lead, not proof: compare any hit with your own baseline before acting. ```
| append [
    | tstats summariesonly=false fillnull_value="-" count min(_time) AS first_seen max(_time) AS last_seen
        FROM datamodel=Endpoint.Filesystem
        WHERE Filesystem.file_name="README_BYOD_*.txt"
        BY Filesystem.dest Filesystem.user Filesystem.file_path Filesystem.file_name Filesystem.action
    | rename "Filesystem.*" AS *
    | eval host=dest, src="-", detail="file ".action." ".file_path, data_model="Endpoint.Filesystem"
    | eval ioc="README_BYOD ransom notes", ioc_type="behaviour (alert)"
]

``` Behaviour 2 (Alert): Mass renames to the .byod extension
    Look for: Many files renamed to the .byod extension on one device or share within minutes
    Log source: EDR file events, file server auditing
    Lead, not proof: compare any hit with your own baseline before acting. ```
| append [
    | tstats summariesonly=false fillnull_value="-" count min(_time) AS first_seen max(_time) AS last_seen
        FROM datamodel=Endpoint.Filesystem
        WHERE Filesystem.file_name="*.byod"
        BY Filesystem.dest Filesystem.user
    | rename "Filesystem.*" AS *
    | eval host=dest, src="-", detail=count." .byod file events", data_model="Endpoint.Filesystem"
    | eval ioc="Mass renames to the .byod extension", ioc_type="behaviour (alert)"
]
| sort 0 - last_seen
| eval first_seen=strftime(first_seen, "%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S"), last_seen=strftime(last_seen, "%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S")
| table first_seen last_seen ioc ioc_type data_model host user src dest detail count

``` Behaviours to check by hand (no reliable Splunk field mapping, so no search is written):

    Behaviour 3 (Hunt): Fake system update shortly before encryption
        Look for: A "system update" notice and a message about deleting old files shortly before encryption
        Log source: Group Policy and scheduled task changes, software deployment logs, EDR process events
        Why no search: no fixed artefact was published; review Group Policy, scheduled task and software deployment changes in the hours before encryption

    Behaviour 4 (Alert): Cloud file deletions by administrators who did not make them
        Look for: Cloud storage notices of file deletions by administrators who did not touch the files
        Log source: Box admin events, OneDrive and SharePoint audit (FileDeleted, FileRenamed)
        Why no search: depends on which administrators normally delete files; review Box admin events and OneDrive or SharePoint FileDeleted by admin accounts against that baseline

    Behaviours are leads, not confirmed indicators, and can match legitimate activity. Compare any hit against your own baseline and environment before acting. Behaviours are for hunting and alerting only; never block on them. ```
